 notes='Atkin page 8 continues (3.11) with P=product p_i^theta_i and deduces positive density of coefficients divisible by each power of 13. It then contrasts the partition function and other forms of half-integral dimension, whose M- and R-results contain an additional unknown constant. The author reports finding thirteen accidents and concludes divisibility of p(n) by 13^13 on a set of positive density. The two occurrences of 13 have different roles, as the source explicitly jokes. Section 4, The cases q=17,19,and 23, reports analogous computations among the primes 17,19,23,29,31,37, using methods cited as ATKIN(1967). It attributes the extension of tests to reusable computer subroutines. No external source was consulted.\n\nS048-POSITIVE-DENSITY: Choose alpha distinct primes p_i other than 13, with theta_i as in the recurrence argument on scan47. Equation (3.7) propagates each zero modulo 13 to the normalized coefficient t_alpha(p_i^theta_i). Coprime multiplicativity obtained by iterating (3.9) then makes t_alpha(Pn) divisible by 13^alpha whenever (n,P)=1. Since c(13^alpha) is a unit, this gives (3.11). The subset of indices m=13^alpha Pn with (n,P)=1 has density phi(P)/(13^alpha P^2)>0, establishing the claimed positive lower density under the stated input identities. This reasoning checks the consequence, not the underlying theorems.\n\nThe source’s for and for all are cancelled, while the alpha after the latter remains visibly live. It is not deleted to repair the prose. The two marginal signs are reproduced as proofreading shapes. The numerical accidents for partitions, the half-integral obstruction and tests for q=17,19,23 remain author reports here.',
